            <div xml:id="d145">

                <pb facs="0320" n="248" sameAs="frus:frus1882/pg248"/>

                <head>No. 20.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Earl Granville to Mr. Lowell.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Foreign Office</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1882-02-02"><hi rendition="#italic">February</hi> 2,
                        1882</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to my letter of the 30th
                    December last, I have been informed by Her Majesty’s secretary of state for the
                    home department that he did not fail to <pb facs="0321" n="249" xml:id="pg249"/>
                    refer to the lord lieutenant of Ireland your communication on the subject of
                    Denis Hayes O’Connor, now in custody under the protection of person and property
                    (Ireland) act, 1881, but that his excellency has expressed his regret that he
                    cannot, consistently with his duty, order the prisoner’s release at present.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>GRANVILLE.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
